</p></fn></author-notes><pub-date pub-type="epub"><day>3</day><month>6</month><year>2015</year></pub-date><pub-date pub-type="collection"><day>15</day><month>8</month><year>2015</year></pub-date><volume>89</volume><issue>16</issue><issue-id pub-id-type="pmc-issue-id">256108</issue-id><fpage>8661</fpage><lpage>8665</lpage><history><date date-type="received"><day>4</day><month>5</month><year>2015</year></date><date date-type="accepted"><day>28</day><month>5</month><year>2015</year></date></history><pub-history><event event-type="pmc-release"><date><day>15</day><month>02</month><year>2016</year></date></event><event event-type="pmc-live"><date><day>15</day><month>02</month><year>2016</year></date></event><event event-type="pmc-released-under-embargo"><date><day>28</day><month>08</month><year>2015</year></date></event><event event-type="pmc-last-change"><date iso-8601-date="2016-02-15 00:08:07.250"><day>15</day><month>02</month><year>2016</year></date></event></pub-history><permissions><copyright-statement>Copyright © 2015, American Society for Microbiology. All Rights Reserved.</copyright-statement><copyright-year>2015</copyright-year><copyright-holder>American Society for Microbiology</copyright-holder></permissions><self-uri xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" content-type="pmc-pdf" xlink:href="zjv8661.pdf"/><self-uri xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" content-type="pdf" xlink:href="zjv01615008661.pdf"/><abstract><p>Influenza A virus PA-X comprises an N-terminal PA endonuclease domain and a C-terminal PA-X-specific domain. PA-X reduces host and viral mRNA accumulation via its endonuclease function. Here, we found that the N-terminal 15 amino acids, particularly six basic amino acids, in the C-terminal PA-X-specific region are important for PA-X shutoff activity. These six basic amino acids enabled a PA deletion mutant to suppress protein expression at a level comparable to that of wild-type PA-X.</p></abstract><counts><fig-count count="4"/><table-count